      {
        "id": 265359,
        "name": "",
        "username": "AmeliaHealYa",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ameliahealya/{size}/7327_2.png",
        "created_at": "2026-04-18T04:01:06.998Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"der_andere_Jan\" data-post=\"22\" data-topic=\"28258\" data-full=\"true\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/der_andere_jan/48/1881_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> der_andere_Jan:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Out of curiosity, what is your take on gravity manipulation?<br>\nMy first thought was to either put it close to magnetism (which is, essentially, Metal-bending) or Telekinesis depending on how it is used.<br>\nCome to think of it, Density control and Flying are not totally off the list either.</p>\n<p>So that makes for another nice set of Powers assembled around a shared concept, and begging to be shifted around for various alternate iterations of the hero, right?</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I think that Telekinesis covers the majority of the potential applications of a “Gravity Manipulation” power.</p>\n<p>I would ask the GM before the game starts if they’ll let you use Telekinesis to create “pure force” that directly “crushes”/“smashes”/etc. your target. If the GM says “No, you can only use Telekinesis to move/throw/lift/drop stuff”, then I would take another power for those direct applications of “pure gravitic force”, most likely Cosmic.</p>\n<p>Either way, supplementing Telekinesis with Metal or with any other Materials power seems redundant to me.</p>\n<p>For gravity-powered personal movement, I would take Flying, Leaping, and/or Wall-Crawling. Those are all easy to justify with “Gravity Manipulation” as a descriptor.</p>\n<p>Either Density Control or a combination of Strength and Vitality would work if you want the character to use the same pseudo-scientific explanation of “accustomed to higher gravity” for enhanced durability and strength as characters like John Carter of Mars or Superman.</p>\n<p><strong>Sidebar:</strong> I once created a character for a superhero RPG who was basically a mash-up of the titular characters from <em>Doctor Who</em> and <em>House, MD</em>, a cranky super-intelligent alien from a “low-gravity” world. They were physically disabled while on Earth, suffering the same mechanical penalties that Earth’s natives would suffer on “high-gravity” worlds, so they had a debilitating addiction to painkillers, and they walked with a cane that they had souped-up into a sci-fi multi-tool and arsenal, like a sonic screwdriver crossed with a <em>Star Trek</em> phaser.</p>",
        "post_number": 24,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2026-04-18T04:01:31.317Z",
