      {
        "id": 265363,
        "name": "Chief Lackey Rich",
        "username": "Chief_Lackey_Rich",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/c/6de8d8/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2026-04-18T12:23:18.071Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Welcome aboard.  Nice to see a new account that’s definitively a human being and not some weird AI thing or simple-minded spambot.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"AmeliaHealYa\" data-post=\"25\" data-topic=\"28258\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ameliahealya/48/7327_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> AmeliaHealYa:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Minor Nitpick: James Robinson’s (excellent) 1990s <em>Starman</em> series and subsequent <em>The Shade</em> miniseries changed The Shade into more of an antihero type.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Sure, but the bulk of his career (and it’s a long one) has cast him as a villain pure and simple, and even as an antihero he’s still on the (ahem) shady side by comic book hero standards.  He didn’t start out as a shadow-wielding hero, or even antihero.</p>\n<p>Similarly, while Cloak is clearly a shadow user (which I wasn’t questioning), his status as a hero was pretty shaky early on.  I haven’t read much of him (or Dagger) since the first year or two of their existence and they’ve probably changed a lot since, but both were more on the “fairly ruthless vigilantes hunting drug dealers” side of things in their origin than squeaky clean moral paragons.</p>\n<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"AmeliaHealYa\" data-post=\"25\" data-topic=\"28258\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ameliahealya/48/7327_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> AmeliaHealYa:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Shadow Lass, AKA “Umbra”, depending on the era.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Still the only shadow-wielding hero-from-day-one I’ve read (The Darkness from Image definitely does not qualify as a hero, and only one iteration of Raven really leans that way over “dark magic user”), but I think it’s worth noting that her shadow powers are just that - she makes darkness.  Unlike the majority of “shadow supers” her power isn’t generally associated with things beyond darkness, no cold or life draining or necromancy or other dark magic stuff, and certainly no portals to other dimensions or teleporting or any of the other tricks that are often part of a package “shadow powers” deal.  It’s a pretty notable difference between her and the rest.</p>\n<p>Whether that’s solely due to many LoSH members having rather narrow, specialized power sets is debatable, but it does keep her from having the kind of creep factor many shadow supers do.  Most of the time she’s clearly heroic without the baggage of (say) Raven or Cloak and his fellow DarkForce users.  The couple of times I’ve seen writers try to tie some dubious mystical stuff to her it hasn’t worked well or stuck around long.</p>",
